{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-340455" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(11x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041565/state.json" "file_sha256" "b422e6c25d857cba3f1563e24159525c37b401d48daf938c44580c831d11c2db"} {"question" "q-340455" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11x-5x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11x+5x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(16x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(11x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(16x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)+\\frac{1}{32}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(16x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Deve